    "begin": {
      "word": "BEGIN",
      "definitions": [
        {
          "text": "To have or commence an independent or first existence; to take rise; to commence. Vast chain of being! which from God began. Pope.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To do the first act or the first part of an action; to enter upon or commence something new, as a new form or state of being, or course of action; to take the first step; to start. \"Tears began to flow.\" Dryden. When I begin, I will also make an end. 1 Sam. iii. 12.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To enter on; to commence. Ye nymphs of Solyma ! begin the song. Pope.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To trace or lay the foundation of; to make or place a beginning of. The apostle begins our knowledge in the creatures, which leads us to the knowledge of God. Locke.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "Beginning. [Poetic & Obs.] Spenser.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        }
